      "change": "Bacterial infections that had killed for all of history became curable.",
      "context": "Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould killed bacteria. A decade later Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and Norman Heatley purified penicillin and proved it in patients; American industry scaled production. Sulfonamide drugs (1935) and streptomycin (1943) broadened the arsenal.",
      "significance": "Surgery, childbirth and minor wounds became far safer, and life expectancy rose worldwide. Overuse has since driven antibiotic resistance, now a global threat.",
